WEBVTT

00:00:00.103 --> 00:00:04.274
A useful way to understand joins is to separate
matching from preservation

00:00:06.011 --> 00:00:09.366
The ON condition decides whether a row from one
table matches a

00:00:09.392 --> 00:00:14.145
row from another table For example an admission
matches a patient when

00:00:14.171 --> 00:00:19.763
the patient IDs are equal The join type answers a
different question

00:00:20.700 --> 00:00:24.529
If no match exists should that unmatched row
disappear or should it

00:00:24.542 --> 00:00:30.192
remain with NULL values from the missing side This
distinction helps explain

00:00:30.206 --> 00:00:33.863
why the same ON condition can produce different
outputs under INNER JOIN

00:00:33.877 --> 00:00:39.551
and LEFT JOIN Also remember that SQL joins combine
rows not just

00:00:39.578 --> 00:00:44.501
unique IDs If one patient has two admissions there
are two valid

00:00:44.528 --> 00:00:50.028
patient-admission combinations The patient
information will appear on both result rows

00:00:50.054 --> 00:00:55.370
because the grain is now admission detail Before
moving on try to

00:00:55.383 --> 00:00:58.988
explain a join in plain language compare these two
row sets match

00:00:59.002 --> 00:01:01.910
them using this rule and preserve these unmatched
rows
